1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,660
An illustration: on the
first of each month,

2
00:00:03,700 --> 00:00:08,025
someone runs a quarterly revenue
report on the production database.

3
00:00:08,065 --> 00:00:10,345
It crawls through rows for many minutes.

4
00:00:10,385 --> 00:00:13,740
Meanwhile, checkout gets
slow for every customer.

5
00:00:14,140 --> 00:00:18,190
Same data, same SQL — so why
does this one query hurt?

6
00:00:18,290 --> 00:00:21,010
By the end, you'll know
where that report belongs,

7
00:00:21,050 --> 00:00:23,870
why its database stores data in columns,

8
00:00:23,910 --> 00:00:27,375
and how answers computed
once come back in seconds.

9
00:00:28,375 --> 00:00:32,840
This video covers the second half of
Chapter 3 of Martin Kleppmann's book,

10
00:00:32,880 --> 00:00:35,670
Designing Data-Intensive Applications.

11
00:00:35,710 --> 00:00:40,090
The previous video, Part 1,
was about storage engines built

12
00:00:40,130 --> 00:00:44,115
for looking things up by key —
logs, LSM-trees, and B-trees.

13
00:00:44,155 --> 00:00:47,590
Our monthly report raises
three questions. First:

14
00:00:47,630 --> 00:00:50,700
why do analytic queries
need their own database?

15
00:00:50,740 --> 00:00:54,170
Second: how should that database
lay its data out on disk?

16
00:00:54,210 --> 00:00:58,290
And third: can we avoid computing
the same answer over and over?

17
00:00:59,190 --> 00:01:04,260
In our story, checkout is online
transaction processing, or OLTP.

18
00:01:04,300 --> 00:01:08,230
The report is online analytic
processing, or OLAP.

19
00:01:08,270 --> 00:01:11,410
A transaction here just
means a quick group of reads

20
00:01:11,450 --> 00:01:14,765
and writes — not necessarily
full ACID guarantees.

21
00:01:14,805 --> 00:01:18,005
Typically, a transaction
reads a few rows by key,

22
00:01:18,045 --> 00:01:21,520
while the report aggregates
over a huge number of rows.

23
00:01:21,560 --> 00:01:25,080
Transactions write small,
random updates as users click;

24
00:01:25,120 --> 00:01:29,485
analytics gets its data through
bulk imports or event streams.

25
00:01:29,525 --> 00:01:31,935
Transactions serve
customers through the app;

26
00:01:31,975 --> 00:01:34,960
analytics serves analysts
making decisions.

27
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:37,680
Transactions work on the
latest state of things;

28
00:01:37,720 --> 00:01:40,710
analytics on the history of
everything that happened.

29
00:01:40,750 --> 00:01:42,550
And the sizes typically differ:

30
00:01:42,590 --> 00:01:46,655
gigabytes to terabytes,
versus terabytes to petabytes.

31
00:01:47,755 --> 00:01:52,670
Put both on one database, and the report
starts a huge scan through the tables.

32
00:01:52,710 --> 00:01:56,778
It competes for disk, memory,
and CPU with the small transactions

33
00:01:56,818 --> 00:02:00,800
customers are waiting on,
so those queue up and slow down.

34
00:02:00,840 --> 00:02:04,680
That's why the people running
OLTP databases are reluctant

35
00:02:04,720 --> 00:02:08,985
to let analysts run ad hoc,
one-off queries on them.

36
00:02:09,025 --> 00:02:13,220
The usual answer is to give
analytics a database of its own.

37
00:02:14,320 --> 00:02:17,414
First stop: data warehousing —
a separate database,

38
00:02:17,454 --> 00:02:21,375
built for answering questions
instead of serving customers.

39
00:02:21,415 --> 00:02:24,040
So how does our report's data get there?

40
00:02:25,340 --> 00:02:29,214
In a real company, the data
behind our report lives in

41
00:02:29,254 --> 00:02:33,550
several OLTP systems: orders,
payments, a support tool.

42
00:02:33,590 --> 00:02:38,575
First, you extract it, with a periodic
dump or a continuous stream of changes.

43
00:02:38,615 --> 00:02:42,120
Then you transform it into a schema
that's friendly to analysis,

44
00:02:42,160 --> 00:02:44,015
and clean it up along the way.

45
00:02:44,055 --> 00:02:46,645
Finally, you load it into
the data warehouse —

46
00:02:46,685 --> 00:02:50,385
a separate copy that analysts
can read but not write.

47
00:02:50,425 --> 00:02:54,370
Extract, transform, load —
together, that's called ETL.

48
00:02:54,410 --> 00:02:59,815
Now the monthly report can scan as much as
it likes without slowing checkout down.

49
00:03:00,915 --> 00:03:03,440
From the outside,
a warehouse looks familiar:

50
00:03:03,480 --> 00:03:07,680
you still talk to it in SQL,
often with the same tools.

51
00:03:07,720 --> 00:03:11,010
But under the lid, the internals
are tuned for a different job —

52
00:03:11,050 --> 00:03:14,125
big scans, not quick lookups by key.

53
00:03:14,425 --> 00:03:20,150
As of the book in 2017, commercial
warehouses included Teradata, Vertica,

54
00:03:20,190 --> 00:03:25,215
SAP HANA, and ParAccel — Amazon
Redshift is a hosted version of it.

55
00:03:25,255 --> 00:03:29,600
And there were open-source SQL-on-Hadoop
engines, like Apache Hive,

56
00:03:29,640 --> 00:03:34,240
Spark SQL, Cloudera Impala,
and Facebook's Presto.

57
00:03:35,140 --> 00:03:38,760
Inside the warehouse, the data
usually sits in a star schema,

58
00:03:38,800 --> 00:03:42,545
built around one big table in
the middle: the fact table.

59
00:03:42,585 --> 00:03:45,240
Each row is one event — here, one payment.

60
00:03:45,280 --> 00:03:49,190
Some columns describe the payment
itself, like amount and fee;

61
00:03:49,230 --> 00:03:52,365
others are keys pointing
into smaller tables.

62
00:03:52,405 --> 00:03:54,830
Those are dimension
tables: which merchant,

63
00:03:54,870 --> 00:03:57,660
who paid, how they paid, where, and when.

64
00:03:57,760 --> 00:03:59,835
Even the date gets its own dimension,

65
00:03:59,875 --> 00:04:03,850
so you can record things like
public holidays and filter on them.

66
00:04:03,890 --> 00:04:06,740
Draw the dimensions around
the fact table like rays,

67
00:04:06,780 --> 00:04:09,350
and you get a star — hence the name.

68
00:04:10,450 --> 00:04:12,250
Real fact tables are wide:

69
00:04:12,290 --> 00:04:16,075
often over a hundred columns,
sometimes several hundred.

70
00:04:16,115 --> 00:04:20,525
And they get huge: the book describes
how a big enterprise may hold

71
00:04:20,565 --> 00:04:24,880
tens of petabytes of transaction
history, most of it in fact tables.

72
00:04:25,180 --> 00:04:30,035
A variation, the snowflake schema,
breaks dimensions into sub-dimensions —

73
00:04:30,075 --> 00:04:33,360
a merchant points to its
category and its country.

74
00:04:33,400 --> 00:04:36,710
That's more normalized, but
stars are often preferred,

75
00:04:36,750 --> 00:04:39,525
because they're simpler
for analysts to work with.

76
00:04:39,565 --> 00:04:43,330
Keep that width in mind — it's
the root of our next problem.

77
00:04:44,230 --> 00:04:48,137
Second stop: column storage.
Our report touches a few columns

78
00:04:48,177 --> 00:04:52,560
of a very wide table — so how
should that table sit on disk?

79
00:04:53,860 --> 00:04:56,770
Here's our report as a
query: sum the amount,

80
00:04:56,810 --> 00:04:59,665
grouped by country,
for last quarter's dates.

81
00:04:59,705 --> 00:05:01,380
Out of a hundred-plus columns,

82
00:05:01,420 --> 00:05:05,460
a typical warehouse query like
this needs just four or five.

83
00:05:05,560 --> 00:05:09,870
But most databases you know, including
the engines from the previous video,

84
00:05:09,910 --> 00:05:14,450
store data row by row, with each
row's values stored together.

85
00:05:14,490 --> 00:05:17,705
So even with an index on the
date, the database loads every

86
00:05:17,745 --> 00:05:22,080
matching row from disk, every
column of it, and parses it.

87
00:05:22,120 --> 00:05:25,010
Then it throws away almost
everything it just read,

88
00:05:25,050 --> 00:05:28,410
keeping only the few values
the report asked for.

89
00:05:29,510 --> 00:05:33,595
Column storage flips this: each
column goes into its own file —

90
00:05:33,635 --> 00:05:38,125
here, six files for twelve payments,
a simplified illustration.

91
00:05:38,165 --> 00:05:40,955
The trick is that every
file keeps the same order,

92
00:05:40,995 --> 00:05:44,695
so the fourth entry in each
file belongs to payment four.

93
00:05:44,895 --> 00:05:48,920
A mini version of our report —
total amount for Germany, DE:

94
00:05:48,960 --> 00:05:51,945
the row store reads all 72 cells,

95
00:05:51,985 --> 00:05:56,450
the column store only the
country and amount files — 24.

96
00:05:56,850 --> 00:06:01,025
Flip it around and fetch all of
payment four: six cells either way,

97
00:06:01,065 --> 00:06:05,255
but rows keep them in one place,
while columns need six files.

98
00:06:05,295 --> 00:06:08,850
So it's a trade-off: columns
win when you scan many rows

99
00:06:08,890 --> 00:06:12,490
but few columns; rows win
when you need whole records.

100
00:06:12,790 --> 00:06:16,105
Careful: column families in
Cassandra and HBase keep a

101
00:06:16,145 --> 00:06:20,205
row's values together,
so they're mostly row-oriented.

102
00:06:21,105 --> 00:06:24,590
Storing by column has a second
benefit: values repeat.

103
00:06:24,630 --> 00:06:27,820
Here's the country column
from our twelve payments.

104
00:06:27,860 --> 00:06:31,025
Only three distinct values
across twelve rows.

105
00:06:31,065 --> 00:06:33,230
The book's example is a
retailer with billions

106
00:06:33,270 --> 00:06:37,670
of sales but only 100,000
distinct products.

107
00:06:37,770 --> 00:06:41,065
So for each value, keep a
bitmap: one bit per row,

108
00:06:41,105 --> 00:06:44,025
set to one wherever
that row has the value.

109
00:06:44,425 --> 00:06:46,985
Each bitmap is just
runs of zeros and ones,

110
00:06:47,025 --> 00:06:49,415
so store how long each run is instead,

111
00:06:49,455 --> 00:06:55,100
counting zeros first:
Germany becomes 0, 3, 6, 2, 1.

112
00:06:55,600 --> 00:06:57,640
That's called run-length encoding.

113
00:06:57,680 --> 00:07:02,397
With many distinct values,
like those 100,000 products,

114
00:07:02,437 --> 00:07:07,345
each bitmap is mostly zeros, and
run-length encoding makes it very compact.

115
00:07:08,445 --> 00:07:11,095
Bitmaps can also answer
questions directly.

116
00:07:11,135 --> 00:07:14,400
Say the report wants
Germany or the Netherlands.

117
00:07:14,440 --> 00:07:19,300
Combine those two bitmaps with a bitwise
OR, and you have every matching row.

118
00:07:19,500 --> 00:07:23,910
Conditions on different columns
work too: German payments made by

119
00:07:23,950 --> 00:07:28,140
card are the Germany bitmap AND the
card bitmap — just payment ten.

120
00:07:28,440 --> 00:07:32,740
That only works because every column
file keeps the rows in the same order —

121
00:07:32,780 --> 00:07:35,610
the same rule that lined up payment four.

122
00:07:36,710 --> 00:07:41,085
Compression pays off twice. When a
warehouse scans millions of rows,

123
00:07:41,125 --> 00:07:47,025
a big bottleneck is moving data from disk
into memory, and from memory into the CPU.

124
00:07:47,065 --> 00:07:49,700
A chunk of compressed
column holds many rows

125
00:07:49,740 --> 00:07:53,245
and fits in the processor's
small, fast L1 cache.

126
00:07:53,285 --> 00:07:57,520
The CPU can then run that bitwise AND
straight over the compressed chunk,

127
00:07:57,560 --> 00:08:01,340
in a tight loop, without calling
a function for every value.

128
00:08:01,380 --> 00:08:03,755
The book calls this vectorized processing,

129
00:08:03,795 --> 00:08:08,900
and SIMD instructions let one
instruction handle many values at once.

130
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:12,770
So far, our rows sit in
the order they arrived,

131
00:08:12,810 --> 00:08:17,245
so the date column jumps around:
twelve runs for twelve rows.

132
00:08:17,285 --> 00:08:20,780
You can choose a sort order,
but not one column on its own —

133
00:08:20,820 --> 00:08:24,160
you'd lose track of which amount
belongs to which payment.

134
00:08:24,200 --> 00:08:28,550
Instead, you sort whole rows, even though
each column is stored separately —

135
00:08:28,590 --> 00:08:31,600
here by date first, then by merchant.

136
00:08:31,800 --> 00:08:36,210
A query for June 2 now reads only
that block and skips the rest —

137
00:08:36,250 --> 00:08:39,035
just what a last-quarter report wants.

138
00:08:39,335 --> 00:08:43,920
Sorting helps compression too: the date
column drops from twelve runs to three;

139
00:08:43,960 --> 00:08:49,350
merchant, sorted only within each day,
has eight; amount stays at twelve.

140
00:08:49,750 --> 00:08:51,810
On billions of rows, the book notes,

141
00:08:51,850 --> 00:08:54,885
that first column could
shrink to a few kilobytes.

142
00:08:55,785 --> 00:08:58,700
Warehouses keep several
copies of the data anyway,

143
00:08:58,740 --> 00:09:01,535
so that losing a machine
doesn't lose data.

144
00:09:01,575 --> 00:09:04,135
That means each copy can
be sorted differently:

145
00:09:04,175 --> 00:09:07,830
one by date, one by
merchant, one by country.

146
00:09:07,870 --> 00:09:10,845
Each query then uses the
copy that fits it best —

147
00:09:10,885 --> 00:09:15,595
an idea from a research system
called C-Store, adopted by Vertica.

148
00:09:15,635 --> 00:09:19,870
Unlike secondary indexes, which only
point to rows stored elsewhere,

149
00:09:19,910 --> 00:09:23,355
each sort order holds the
actual column values.

150
00:09:24,355 --> 00:09:27,990
All this tidiness has a cost,
though, when new payments arrive.

151
00:09:28,030 --> 00:09:31,685
Compressed columns can't be
updated in place like a B-tree,

152
00:09:31,725 --> 00:09:34,020
and a new row that sorts
into the middle would

153
00:09:34,060 --> 00:09:37,460
most likely mean rewriting
every column file.

154
00:09:37,760 --> 00:09:41,540
The fix comes from the LSM-trees
in the previous video:

155
00:09:41,580 --> 00:09:44,970
new writes go into a sorted
buffer in memory first.

156
00:09:45,010 --> 00:09:48,084
When enough have piled up,
they're merged with the column

157
00:09:48,124 --> 00:09:51,280
files on disk and written
out as new files, in bulk.

158
00:09:51,320 --> 00:09:54,560
Queries read both the files and
the buffer and combine them,

159
00:09:54,600 --> 00:09:58,635
so new data shows up right away —
Vertica works this way.

160
00:09:59,635 --> 00:10:03,910
Third stop: precomputed aggregates.
The monthly report adds up the same

161
00:10:03,950 --> 00:10:08,540
kind of totals every month — can
we do that work once and reuse it?

162
00:10:09,840 --> 00:10:13,510
Warehouse queries keep asking
for the same aggregates —

163
00:10:13,550 --> 00:10:17,620
counts, sums, averages, minimums,
maximums — over and over.

164
00:10:17,660 --> 00:10:21,820
A regular, virtual view doesn't
help: it's just a saved query

165
00:10:21,860 --> 00:10:25,155
that gets expanded and rerun
every time you read it.

166
00:10:25,195 --> 00:10:27,310
A materialized view is different:

167
00:10:27,350 --> 00:10:32,395
it stores an actual copy of the result
on disk, so reading it is cheap.

168
00:10:32,695 --> 00:10:35,770
The catch: whenever the
underlying data changes,

169
00:10:35,810 --> 00:10:40,455
the view has to be updated too,
which makes every write more expensive.

170
00:10:40,495 --> 00:10:44,230
That's why they're not often
used in OLTP databases,

171
00:10:44,270 --> 00:10:48,060
but in a read-heavy warehouse
the trade is often worth it.

172
00:10:48,960 --> 00:10:52,905
A common special case is the
data cube: a grid of totals,

173
00:10:52,945 --> 00:10:55,125
here revenue by date and merchant,

174
00:10:55,165 --> 00:10:58,680
in thousands of dollars,
from a larger set of payments.

175
00:10:58,720 --> 00:11:00,900
It's a simplified illustration.

176
00:11:00,940 --> 00:11:04,640
Add up a row and you get one
day's total; add up a column

177
00:11:04,680 --> 00:11:08,855
and you get one merchant's
total — 700 thousand overall.

178
00:11:09,155 --> 00:11:13,590
Real fact tables have more dimensions,
so the grid grows into a cube,

179
00:11:13,630 --> 00:11:17,245
and with five dimensions,
a five-dimensional hypercube.

180
00:11:17,285 --> 00:11:21,400
Back in our grid, Bolt's revenue
across all days is a single lookup:

181
00:11:21,440 --> 00:11:24,980
255 thousand, already computed.

182
00:11:25,280 --> 00:11:29,330
But ask about payments over 100
dollars, and the cube can't help,

183
00:11:29,370 --> 00:11:32,020
because amount isn't
one of its dimensions.

184
00:11:32,060 --> 00:11:35,590
That's why most warehouses keep
as much raw data as they can,

185
00:11:35,630 --> 00:11:39,030
and treat cubes as a
speed-up for common queries.

186
00:11:40,130 --> 00:11:44,485
Let's go back to the first of the month,
and that report slowing down checkout.

187
00:11:44,525 --> 00:11:49,155
Checkout got slow because analytics
and transactions shared one database;

188
00:11:49,195 --> 00:11:52,215
ETL into a warehouse separates them.

189
00:11:52,255 --> 00:11:55,569
The report read whole rows;
a column store reads only

190
00:11:55,609 --> 00:11:59,400
the columns it needs,
compressed and sorted by date.

191
00:11:59,500 --> 00:12:02,000
And the same totals,
recomputed every month,

192
00:12:02,040 --> 00:12:05,105
can come from a materialized
view or a cube —

193
00:12:05,145 --> 00:12:08,070
an answer in seconds, not many minutes.

194
00:12:09,370 --> 00:12:11,865
Now, some questions for your own system.

195
00:12:11,905 --> 00:12:13,580
Where do your heavy reports run —

196
00:12:13,620 --> 00:12:18,460
on the database serving your users,
or on a copy built for analysis?

197
00:12:18,560 --> 00:12:23,260
Which of your queries scan millions
of rows but use only a few columns?

198
00:12:23,360 --> 00:12:25,175
And which totals do you recompute on every

199
00:12:25,215 --> 00:12:28,445
dashboard load that
could be computed once?

200
00:12:29,545 --> 00:12:32,785
Next is Chapter 4, Encoding and Evolution:

201
00:12:32,825 --> 00:12:36,950
how data written by one version of
your code gets read by another.

202
00:12:36,990 --> 00:12:40,830
We'll look at formats like JSON,
Protocol Buffers, and Avro.

203
00:12:40,870 --> 00:12:43,643
Martin Kleppmann's book
and the blog presentation

204
00:12:43,683 --> 00:12:46,715
are credited on screen.
Thanks for watching.
